    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "where A: [0,∞] → [0,∞] is a Young function. A function A: [0,∞] → [0,∞] is called a Young function if it is convex, left-continuous, vanishing at 0, and not constant on the entire interval (0,∞). For example, when p ∈ [1,∞), we have  · Lp(R,µ) =  · LA(R,µ) with A(t) = tp, t ≥ 0. We also have  · L∞(R,µ) =  · LA(R,µ) with A(t) = ∞ · χ(1,∞](t), t ≥ 0. Besides the classical textbooks [6, 53], the interested reader can ﬁnd more information on the contemporary theory of Orlicz spaces and in particular Orlicz–Sobolev spaces in [16, 46]."
